[Applause]  thank you  we are a president they are Ursula they  are students participants of the  European diplomatic Academy it is for me  really an honor a pleasure an emotion I  would say to welcome the president of  the European commission with us uh when  I heard of this possibility just a few  days ago I was really excited about this  and even if normally during the exam  session we avoid distracting you uh we  thought that this was an excellent  destruction  and an opportunity not to be missed and  I was impressed and I shared this with  the president that in six minutes you  all registered at 8pm so it means that  you were on your laptops which means you  were studying probably but it also means  that you understood why you're here  you're here to study and to study hard  and by the way good luck for your exams  your ongoing session uh some of most  most of you have exams tomorrow too so  good luck for that you're here to study  Europe but you're also here to leave  Europe and to experience how it is that  institutions shape the policies  in some cases we're lucky enough to give  some hints of some contributions some  ideas uh  but I think this is an excellent  opportunity to see the policy making in  the making and we are honored today to  listen from the president of the  European commission  how Europe's competitiveness is going to  develop in a new context  accelerating the green transition with a  strong industrial base which is  something quite complex to tackle but so  important for our continent in the  present and in the future I'm very  excited to have today with us the  President also on a personal level for  me it's a pleasure always to see you  again we shared so many important  moments in a couple of lives ago and I  can testimony anytime over dedication  for wisdom the energy that she puts in  everything she does and I always tell  her when we have the occasion when I  have the pleasure to meet her that I am  always glad and relieved to know that in  these difficult times we have someone  like her like leading the European  commission so also place the Flores  yourself thank you for being here with  us  foreign  promotion  and dear young diplomats and most of all  thank you very much dear Federica  um thank you very much for making it  possible that I speak here today and  that you invited me and let me thank you  very much for what you're doing here in  Bruges  because indeed when we first met you  were the high representative I was the  German defense minister there was  something that struck me immediately  meeting you and speaking to you because  besides the day-to-day crisis that you  had to manage  you were almost always somebody who  tried to focus on the long-term  strategic thinking that was always  important for you and you always thought  that Europe needed a common strategic  culture and in the very end it resulted  in the global strategy that you  developed it was a process to help a  European thinking emerge and it led to  very practical steps towards a common  European Defense basis that we worked on  together now you're doing the same here  in Bruges with young men and women from  across Europe and with young diplomats  from all member states and Beyond and I  really want to congratulate you the  young students here in this pilot and  all the participants of the European  diplomatic Academy because you are  basically the future of the European  foreign policy  so many thanks for having me here today  what we need is indeed strategic  thinking more than ever  because we are in difficult times  and these leads of course to difficult  questions  what is Europe's strategic goal  what should our economy look like in the  2030s and Beyond what is our growth  strategy  what are the challenges to address in  the short term the midterm and the long  term  there's one common feature  in answering  all these questions  and that is without any doubt the big  topic of climate change  its consequences will come in all shapes  and all forms  and it is the most existential threat  and issue we all face  this is why the European Union has a  very clear road map for this challenge  you all know it's called our European  green deal  it is our new and sustainable growth  strategy  and when we set off  we knew that the road would not always  be smooth  that as the global setting around us  is constantly changing  we would have to constantly adapt to  stay the course  but let me tell you we did not know how  bumpy this road would be  we had a pandemic with aftershocks that  are still being felt around the world  across the world  business business has a tough time to  really  catch up again after this pandemic and  the lockdowns Russia Unleashed an  unprovoked unjustified brutal atrocious  war against Ukraine  and the spillover effects are Global  from energy supply to the redrawing of  global diplomacy  and now and that's the reason why I'm  here today we see in parallel that we  are confronted with an intensifying  Global clean tech race  in tackling all these new challenges  we always have to keep in mind the  direction of our travel  it is towards a thriving  a clean and a competitive European  economy  and this is what I want to talk about  today to you  if we want to speak about that let us  first zoom out a little bit  and bring you back in time a few years  it is exactly three years ago that we  launched the European green deal  it was the very beginning of my mandate  as president of the European Commission  and we decided that our first priority  would be  to decarbonize  and digitalize the European economy  I called it our man in the moon moment  and it was really moonshot  we were the first Global power to set  the path towards net zero emissions  and to make it legally binding  you might remember at that time we had  in the United States an Administration  in place that did not believe that  humans caused climate change  and announced to pull out of the Paris  agreement  China was heavily investing in coal  and Europe emerged as a global  front-runner  we said to ourselves let's not wait to  do what is right  let's invest today  in the clean Technologies of tomorrow  because eventually if we do it right all  advanced and emerging eCommerce  economies will embark on the same  Journey  and we've stayed the course ever since  this has given certainty it has given  certainty to our industry to our  investors on the direction of travel  we have cast our goals in the first ever  climate law  when the pandemic hit we created Next  Generation EU not only to power the  recovery that was also important  but also to accelerate the  digitalization and the European green  deal  just keep in mind that almost half of  Next Generation you this is our 800  billion recovery plan half of it  contributes to a wider climate  objectives  we developed a legal framework to cut  emissions by at least 55 percent  and in parallel we have worked with  industry and strategic fields from joint  European projects on batteries or  hydrogen to the European ships Act  Europe has become the Global Hub of  investment for cleantech and  decarbonization  now if you now fast forward to today  first of all there is some positive news  Europe is no longer alone in the fight  against climate change  when President Biden came into office  one of his first decisions was to rejoin  the Paris agreement  and now the United States has taken the  next step  and passed the inflation reduction Act  and the bipartisan infrastructure law  the inflation reduction act called IRA  is an investment plan of roughly  369 billion dollars  to build up a new industrial ecosystem  in strategic clean energy sectors  and there is a striking symmetry  between the inflation reduction Act  and our European green deal  both of them  are simultaneously  a climate strategy  and a strategy for investment and growth  both of them  include funding for just transition  and both of them include regulatory  standards so we have the two biggest and  most advanced economies in the world  that are now moving in the same  direction  the United States relies traditionally  on investment and Regulatory standards  while we the European Union relies on a  combination  of investment and Regulatory standards  and carbon pricing  two continent-sized powers  a modernizing the economies  by investing close to a trillion euros  and addressing together the biggest  challenges of our times and this is so  important  it is not only a positive signal for our  planet  but also for our Global Investors  yet  the inflation reduction Act is also  raising concerns  here in Europe  against a very particular backdrop for  our industry and economy  the first point is that covet has  exacerbated bottlenecks in many critical  Supply chains  the European industry has been carefully  trying to fix the weak Links of these  change in the last weeks and months and  make them more resilient  but of course this has come at a cost  the second element you are very familiar  with  that is the global energy Supply is  tight  because of Russia's war of aggression  you know it from the European Union just  think of the fact that in the last eight  months only Russia has cut 80 percent of  the pipeline gas supply to the European  Union  but and this is important  we've been able to compensate  something that is truly remarkable  eighty percent of the pipeline gas  supply in eight months  it is remarkable and it deemed  impossible to many  but we should keep in mind in these  eight months there have been not only no  blackouts in the European Union  but we also managed to fill our gas  storages by almost 96 so we are safe for  this winter  but this comes also  at a higher cost of energy for our  industry and our smes  and all of this  affects the competitiveness  of many European Industries especially  energy intensive sectors of our economy  it is against this spectrop  that the inflation reduction Act  has received so much additional scrutiny  in Europe and also in the rest of the  world  there's a risk  that the IRA  can lead to unfair competition  could close markets  and that's fragment the very same  critical Supply chains that have already  been tested by covet  so we need to look at these issues  closely and at the same time learn what  we can do better  and in doing so we need to look at three  aspects that are particularly  challenging in the ira  the first one is  by American  this by American logic  underpins part of the IRA  second difficult issue is tax breaks  that could lead to discrimination some  get them others not  and the search element is production  subsidies  that could lead to a subsidies race  now this sounds all very technical  but it's easier to understand if I give  you one example  if you are a consumer in the United  States  you get a tax break when you buy  electric vehicles EVS  if they were manufactured in North  America  and if you are a battery producer  for those same electric vehicles  you get a tax break if you produce in  the United States  so you get the essence this means that a  car manufacturer gets a double benefit  for producing in North America  and for buying parts in the United  States  and what is more is that this could also  attract critical components and raw  materials towards the United States and  away from the transatlantic Supply  chains  and you can imagine this creates of  course  first of all an attractive conducive  investment environment on clean tech in  the United States  but we already see how this could also  affect Europe's own clean tech Base by  redirecting investment flows  and we've all heard the stories of  producers that are considering to  relocate future investments from the  European Union to the United States  now I want to be very clear on one point  competition is good  we like it it's important  it drives innovation  it enhances efficiency  it ensures progress  and in doing so it brings down prices  for clean Technologies  competition between Europe and the  United States can push both our  Industries to excel  to innovate to transform faster and all  this is necessary  and this is why I believe in the need to  invest into two clean energy industrial  bases on both sides of the Atlantic  that is so important that we see the  Dual approach not only will we create  the well-paid jobs of the future if we  do so both in the European Union and in  the United States  but we will also drive through this  competition down costs for clean energy  Technologies globally  so together the United States and the  European Union we can make clean energy  more affordable worldwide if we work  together  and by doing so  we will help of course decarbonize  other economies  and drive a just transition  but  you expected a butt in all this  but this competition must respect a  Level Playing Field  and that's why it is so critical  that the technology competition between  the European Union and the United States  is a race to the top for our Industries  on both sides of the Atlantic  and I'm confident that Europe is in a  strong position to compete on global  markets  and develop the clean Technologies of  tomorrow  now will this be enough to keep up the  clean tech race  yes if we are competing  on a Level Playing Field  so we must take action to rebalance the  playing field where the IRA and other  measures create distortions  in other words  we need to do our homework here in  Europe  and at the same time we have to work  with the United States  to mitigate competitive disadvantages  and I see three ways to do so  the first is  we have to adjust our own rules to  facilitate public investment into the  transition  the second  we have to reassess the need for further  European funding of this transition  and thirdly  we have to work with the United States  to address some of the most concerning  aspects of their law  to my first point  as I said we have to adjust our own  rules to make it easier for public  investment to power this transition  State eight State eight is a tried and  tested to World here in Europe  and it incentivizes business activities  for the public interest  just to give you an example last summer  we approved 4.4 billion euros 5.4  billion euros in state 8 for the  hydrogen valid chain  under one of our so-called ipcis you  know the abbreviations important  projects of common European interest  this public investment will benefit 35  companies  from 15 different member States from  Portugal to Denmark from Finland to  Italy  and they will help bring new  technologies from the laboratory to the  factory for hydrogen production for  powered trucks for storage for hydrogen  trains or hydrogen-powered ships  and this is only one of the many  examples of our stated policy at the  service of cleantech  yet the inflection inflation reduction  act should make us reflect  on how we can improve our state aid  framework  and adapt it  to a new global environment  so we must look at how we can make our  framework more predictable  and more simple  Europe as you know has built a very  sophisticated system  but business today is asking for simple  system and simple rules  Europe has built a very precise system  but business today once stayed at rules  to be predictable above all  we are very careful to avoid distortions  in our single Market it's very important  but we also must be responsive to the  increasing Global competition on  cleantech so we have to look at the  global market  and if you look at the inflation  reduction Act  it invests right along the value chain  in some strategic sectors  and this is not always the case with our  state aid  our ipcis for example aim to bring  breakthrough Technologies from the  laboratory to their first industrial  deployment that is good  but we now will also take a fresh look  at how we can support the whole value  chain  down to the mass production of the most  strategic Green Tech Solutions  and clean end products  including through public investment  our stated framework exists to preserve  our very precious single Market  but if we see that investment in  strategic sectors  are leaking away from the European Union  this would only undermine our single  market and that is why  we are now reflecting on how to simplify  and adapt our state aid rules  my second point  my second Point has to do with the  complementary European funding  while it is critical that member states  have the flexibility to invest their  budgets in strategic sectors  this approach cannot be self-standing  because you can imagine if it's only  about State 8  it would of course favor those member  states that have Deep Pockets the fiscal  space  and that would lead to Distortion of our  single market and would eventually  undermine our single Market  thus we also need a common European  answer to the challenge both in the  short term and in the long term  and in the short term  what we have to do is we have to bridge  the difficult time for our smes and  industries right now  to transition to transition towards  cheaper and renewable energy all across  the European Union at the moment being  they're stuck in this energy crunch with  expensive fossil fuels and we have to  support them or Bridge the time so that  they can transform and transition  towards renewable energy that is the  cheap energy the Homegrown and  affordable energy  and here repower EU is our tool  repower issue we are just having it in  the final negotiations in Parliament and  Council to be approved till the end of  the year  repower EU invests in Energy Efficiency  in renewable energy massively  and in the infrastructure that you need  to integrate their European Union the  interconnectors the grid for example  and what is important  repower EU will massively speed up the  permitting process for renewable energy  processes projects  you might know that sometimes these  projects needs seven to eight years to  be permitted so our ambition is now to  reduce that to below one year to really  have all these energy renewable energy  projects that are on the pipeline up and  running  we are now working  on boosting  repower issue  repower EU needs greater Firepower  to accelerate exactly this green  transition however  we also need to look Beyond ad hoc  Solutions  the new assertive industrial policy of  our competitors  needs a structural answer from the  European Union  and in my State of the Union Address  I introduced the idea of establishing a  sovereignty fund  the logic behind the sovereignty fund is  simple  we need a common European industrial  policy and this common European  industrial policy requires common  European funding  the goal of our European industrial  policy  is for European industry to be the  leaders in the clean tech transition  and this means over the midterm  that we have to beef up the resources  available  for Upstream research for innovation  and for strategic projects at the  European Union's level  in other words  this means on one hand  new and additional funding at the EU  level  and it means on the other hand  higher level of policy coordination like  hydrogen like semiconductors Quantum  Computing artificial intelligence  biotechnology all these important topics  and on my third and final point  cooperation  instead of Confrontation  we are working very closely with the  Biden Administration on how to jointly  strengthen strengthen our clean energy  industrial base  and let me go back to the example I gave  you earlier the electric vehicles the  EVS  in Europe we have just agreed to phase  out the combustion engine by 2035.  this means new vehicles sold after 2035  will be exclusively Electric  similar regulatory commitments exist in  the United States  and at the same time  China is subsidizing massively this  sector  so we the United States and the European  Union have a vast common interest to  preserve our industrial leadership  and this will require fundamental  changes in the automobile industry  it will require significant private and  public investments in innovation in  infrastructure in Supply chains and raw  materials on both sides of the Atlantic  but it is not just about investment  it is also about setting standards and  joining forces where it makes sense  take for example the whole topic of the  charging infrastructure for EVS  if Europe and the United States agree on  common standards  it is us who will shape Global standards  and not leave it to others  or take the topic of critical raw  materials for cleantech that we urgently  need  today the production and processing of  some of these critical raw materials  without them no green transition  today the processing and production  is basically controlled by one single  country  and that is China  Europe and the United States can build  an alternative to this Monopoly we see  by establishing a critical raw materials  Club  the idea behind it is simple  it is cooperation with partners and  allies  on the sourcing on the production on the  processing  that gives us the ability to overcome  this Monopoly  so there you have two examples of  standards  or the raw materials  to give you an idea what we are working  on with the White House exactly right  now  and finally  all this should be seen in a wider  context  let us zoom out again and focus on the  bigger picture  an aggressive Russia is threatening our  democracies  and is blackmailing us with our  dependency on fossil fuels  an increasingly assertive China  is cultivating dependencies in all  continents  to project power of its own interests  but by contrast  look at what the United States and  Europe are able to achieve if we join  forces  take Ukraine  think of the immense impact of our  sanctions  think of how much support we have  mobilized together  from our countries and the world  to help Ukraine in the last nine months  this Brave and courageous people  look at us joining forces to dry out  Russia's War chest  by just now introducing a Russian oil  price cap globally  let me tell you that since the end of  the Cold War never never has the  transatlantic Corporation been closer  as in those two last years  we have ended long-standing disputes on  steel tariffs on Airbus Boeing on  securing data flows  we have created a tech and trade Council  to cooperate on Tech and trade mattress  we have set up this spring a task force  on European energy security  that led to additional deliveries of 15  billion cubic meters of LNG this year  and remember I told you that 80 of the  pipeline gas has been cut  so in the first half of 2022  the United States supplied more than  three quarters three quarters of the  eu's additional needs  and on climate  we have brought together over 100  countries to sign the global methane  pledge  a commitment to cut Global methane  emissions by 30 percent in this decade  these are just examples what we are able  to do if we join forces the power of  transatlantic partnership  and therefore these students  ladies and gentlemen  this is my message to you  as you embark on this journey  at the European diplomatic Academy or  students here in Borussia  there will always be times  where new challenges emerge  and old tensions  rise back to the surface  this can be the case with rivals  and competitive  or even with your oldest and closest  partners  but as you will learn in this next year  Europe will always look for Solutions  solutions that are crafted by  cooperation  and designed by diplomacy  and of course Europe always do what is  right for Europe  so yes the European Union will respond  in an adequate and well-calibrated  manner to the inflation reduction Act  but does this mean that we will engage  in a costly trade war with the United  States in the middle of an actual War  this is not in our interest  and nor in the interest of The Americans  and it would harm Global Innovation too  so that is the reason why we're working  so hard in Europe and the United States  now  to exactly precisely address these  distortions  in the last two years  the EU and the United States have shown  that we are stronger individually  When We Stand Together collectively  when we focus on what binds us  our values  our friendship  our belief in fear competition  our belief in open markets  and of course our belief and commitment  to the rules-based order  for friends like us  competition and cooperation can be two  phases of the same coin  so let us strengthen clean investment on  both sides of the Atlantic  let us do it for our people  for Industries  of course for affordable clean energy  worldwide  and for the sake of our planet thank you  very much for listening and Long Live  Europe  foreign

